course: dictionary
lesson: 6998
title: One idea at a time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A paragraph is a group of sentences about one main idea. When the idea changes, the writer starts a new paragraph. Look for the change: a new time, a new place, or a new speaker.
explain: A new paragraph starts on a new line. It is shown by an indent, which is a small gap at the start of the line, or by a blank line. Schools and books use one or the other, so check which one your class uses.
explain: The first paragraph of a news article usually tells who, what, when and where: «Nine-year-old Ella Brown rescued six ducklings from a drain on Monday.» Later paragraphs add more detail, such as quotes like «"It was terrifying," said Mrs Patel».
fact: A paragraph is a group of sentences about one main idea.
fact: A new paragraph starts on a new line, often with an indent: a small gap at the start of the line.
fact: A new paragraph can also begin when the time, the place or the speaker changes.
fact: The first paragraph of a news article usually tells who, what, when and where.
fact: Twice in this part a paragraph begins «Down, down, down.» to show how long the fall goes on.
exercise: What is a paragraph? | A single word with a full stop | *A group of sentences about one main idea | A picture with a caption | A title at the top of a page
exercise: How is a new paragraph shown? | Every word in capitals | *An indent or a blank line | A drawing in the margin | A big number on the page
exercise: Why does a paragraph begin «Down, down, down.»? | To show a new character is speaking | To show a list is coming | *To show how long the fall goes on | To show the story has ended
test: What does the first paragraph of a news article usually tell you? | What the writer had for lunch | How the story ends | Only the weather | *Who, what, when and where
test: Why does a writer start a new paragraph? | The writer has run out of ink | Every line must be longer than the last | To make the page look empty | *The main idea changes, or a new time, place or speaker begins
exam: What makes a group of sentences a paragraph? | It is always longer than a page | *It is about one main idea and starts on a new line | It has a picture beside it | It has exactly five sentences
